---
title: "Global 2017-2025 v1.1 embeddings now available in geotessera 0.11.0"
date: 2026-10-05
tags: [announcement, release, python]
author: "Anil Madhavapeddy"
description: "GeoTessera 0.11 defaults to a complete global run of v1.1 embeddings, streamed straight from AWS. Open the dataset, point it at a region, and read."
---

With [GeoTessera 0.11](https://pypi.org/project/geotessera/), global embeddings
for the whole terrestrial surface of the planet are now available for every year
from 2017 through to 2025! These are available for use straight away, with no
separate registration step required.

## What changes for you?

- You don't need to request coverage any more! The embeddings for your region already
  exist. Ask us only if you want the v2 beta embeddings, which is only for specialised research at this point.
- You can download only what you need, due to Zarr and Icechunk. The `GeoTesseraZarr()` Python class fetches just the points or
  areas you ask for. It streams from AWS over HTTPS, with no sign-up or credentials needed.
- Your workflow can stay the same. Most geotessera download commands work as before, so if you were using
  v1.0 then v1.1 is a dropin improvement (see the [v1.1 announcement](/blog/2026-06-09-tessera-v1-1)). Just be careful
  not to mix embedding versions within the same analysis.

```bash
pip install -U geotessera
geotessera download --bbox 0.05,52.15,0.20,52.25 --output tiles
```

Run `geotessera info` to list every dataset. It marks `v1.1-dclimate (Zarr)` as
the default.

## Collaborating embeddings generation

Our community member [dClimate](https://blog.dclimate.net/dclimate-collaborates-with-aws-to-bring-tesseras-global-land-intelligence-to-the-world/)
and AWS ran the model over the whole world to make this dataset possible. The data is now stored on
[AWS Open Data](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/publicsector/tessera-global-earth-observation-embeddings-now-available-on-aws-open-data/)
and the [Source Cooperative](https://source.coop/tessera/tessera/), and so you dont need to pay any download fees (but please do be reasonable about your use).

dClimate also publish the data in a format called [Icechunk](https://icechunk.io) which has more
advanced features. We [converted](https://anil.recoil.org/notes/2026w38) to plain Zarr v3 for hosting on Source Coop to maximise the 
accessibility.  GeoTessera 0.11 reads both so you don't have to worry about it unless you are running into some performance tuning needs, and more [technical details](https://anil.recoil.org/notes/2026w40) explain how the two formats compare.

Key points are:

- The Cambridge v1.1 run is a separate (and incomplete) embeddings dataset, so we keep it separate from dClimate's.
- We are phasing out NPY tile downloads now that we have global streamed reads over Zarr. Tell us if you still need NPY.
- Older versions and variants, including v1.0, still work and we aim to maintain that archive for as long as we can.

Enjoy v1.1, and as always [feel free to share](https://eeg.zulipchat.com) your usage or ask questions on our community channels!
